### Bug fix

**You own this task. Plan, review, verify.** Delegate investigation and the fix to subagents, stay in the lead. Pick each subagent's reasoning level for the job, per the Subagents section.

Be scientific. Every shipped line traces to runtime evidence. Belt-and-suspenders that "might help" is a hypothesis, not a fix. It does not ship. When evidence refutes a hypothesis, revert what it motivated. The smallest change the evidence justifies ships, nothing more.

1. Reproduce it yourself on the matching surface via the driver skill (Non-negotiables), even when a debug or instrumentation protocol says to ask the user to reproduce. Ask the user only with a stated, specific reason the control surface cannot reach the target, and only after driving it as far as it goes. If it won't reproduce directly, synthesize the trigger, tighten conditions, or instrument until it fires.
2. Binary-search the cause. Form the candidate hypotheses, then rule them out until one survives. Seed them with `how` over the affected subsystem and the **why** skill for regression history. Each pass, take the split that cuts the most remaining problem space, get runtime evidence, eliminate. When program state is unclear, add instrumentation or logging and read it as the code runs. Don't guess. Drive a long or stubborn hunt with Claude Code's `loop` skill. Confirm the surviving *mechanism* with runtime evidence before the step-3 architect/interrogate fan-out.
3. Plan the fix. If it crosses a function boundary, `architect` first. Delegate implementation to a subagent using your configured bug-fix model (default in poteto-mode's Models section) with a specific scope.
4. Verify on the same surface. The original repro now passes. "Inconclusive" or wrong-surface is not a pass. Flag it. Unit tests show branch behavior, not bug absence.
5. Stage the commits so the failing repro lands before the fix in git history. See the **tdd** skill for the failing-test-first cadence when the bug has a cheap local test path. Skip it when the test would be expensive, integration-heavy, or unclear.
   This is the canonical **sequence-verifiable-units** principle skill, the failing test first and the fix on top.
6. Run **Opening a PR**.

**Reply:** what was broken, root cause, fix, how you verified. Quote the decisive failing and passing output, trimmed to the assertion and the counts.
# Composition recipes

Each example is a complete Svelte component placed at `src/Example.svelte`. Load `../lib/ui/styles.css` once in `src/routes/+layout.svelte`. These examples use static presentation data; production features supply real URLs and formatted values.

Actions and focus remain neutral in every entity context. Override `--accent` only for entity meaning, such as funder badges, highlights and charts.

## Search result

`ResultRow` renders `li`. The caller owns the anchor, metadata and list container. The UI stylesheet owns `.entity-text`, `.entity-name` and `.entity-alias`.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { ResultRow, Badge, IconIndicator } from "./lib/ui";
</script>

<ul class="results-list">
  <ResultRow label="Example Foundation, funder">
    <IconIndicator icon="building" label="Organisation" />
    <div class="entity-text">
      <a
        class="entity-name"
        href="/funders/11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111"
        >Example Foundation</a
      >
      <span class="entity-alias">Also recorded as Example Trust</span>
    </div>
    <Badge>Organisation</Badge>
  </ResultRow>
</ul>
```

## Declaration and value

`DeclarationList` renders `ol`, `DeclarationCard` renders `li`, and `ValueRow` renders a nested `li`. `.funding-list` is the UI list recipe for reset and spacing. The registered snippet is already wrapped in `p`, so supply inline content. The UI stylesheet owns `.payment-type` and `.amount`; the caller supplies the formatted amount.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { DeclarationList, DeclarationCard, ValueRow } from "./lib/ui";
</script>

<DeclarationList label="Example declarations">
  <DeclarationCard>
    {#snippet heading()}<h3>Employment and earnings</h3>{/snippet}
    {#snippet registered()}<time datetime="2026-01-20">20 January 2026</time
      >{/snippet}
    <ul class="funding-list">
      <ValueRow>
        {#snippet source()}
          <a href="/funders/11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111"
            >Example Foundation</a
          >
          <span class="payment-type">Consultancy</span>
        {/snippet}
        {#snippet value()}<span class="amount">£1,234.56</span>{/snippet}
      </ValueRow>
    </ul>
  </DeclarationCard>
</DeclarationList>
```

## Identity and metric

`IdentityHeader` owns its title, metadata paragraph and grid. Supply inline metadata and a native navigation composition. `.identity-totals` is caller-owned grid placement. `Metric` takes exact formatted strings without interpreting money.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import {
    IdentityHeader,
    Badge,
    Icon,
    Metric,
    ViewLinks,
    ViewLink,
  } from "./lib/ui";
</script>

<IdentityHeader name="Jane Example">
  {#snippet kind()}<Badge variant="profile"><Icon name="parliament" />MP</Badge
    >{/snippet}
  {#snippet metadata()}Example Party<span>Example constituency</span>{/snippet}
  {#snippet values()}
    <div class="identity-totals">
      <Metric variant="expanded" label="Declared amounts" value="£1,234.56" />
    </div>
  {/snippet}
  {#snippet navigation()}
    <ViewLinks label="Example views">
      <ViewLink href="/members/1" aria-current="page">Summary</ViewLink>
      <ViewLink href="/members/1/history">Declaration history</ViewLink>
    </ViewLinks>
  {/snippet}
</IdentityHeader>

<style>
  .identity-totals {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    min-width: 0;
  }
</style>
```

## Native table columns

`DataTable` supplies the caption, `thead`, `tbody` and neutral cell rules. Use actual `th`/`td` classes in the caller for widths and numeric alignment. [FunderSummary](../../FunderSummary.svelte) is the production example. It owns `.recipient-name` and `.recipient-total`; those classes are not reusable UI APIs.
